Problem Name: Subsets

Intuition
A power set of a set of size n contains 2^n subsets because every element can either be included or excluded. We can systematically generate every valid subset by building paths incrementally using backtracking, saving the current state at every decision point and exploring all remaining elements.

Approach
Use depth-first search (DFS) with backtracking:

* Start with an empty subset path.
* At each recursive step, add a copy of the current path to the result list ans because every prefix forms a valid subset.
* Iterate through the remaining available numbers starting from index start to avoid duplicate combinations and enforce order.
* Include the current element in path, recurse for the next index (i + 1), and then pop the element to backtrack and explore other branches.

Algo

1. Define a helper function backtrack(start, nums, path, ans):
a. Append the current path to ans.
b. Loop index i from start to nums.size() - 1:
* Add nums[i] to path.
* Recursively call backtrack(i + 1, nums, path, ans).
* Remove the last element from path (backtrack).


2. In the main function subsets:
a. Initialize an empty 2D array ans and a 1D array path.
b. Call backtrack(0, nums, path, ans).
c. Return ans.

Code

class Solution {
public:
void backtrack(int start, vector& nums, vector& path,
vector<vector>& ans) {
ans.push_back(path);
for (int i = start; i < nums.size(); ++i) {
path.push_back(nums[i]);
backtrack(i + 1, nums, path, ans);
path.pop_back();
}
}

```
vector<vector<int>> subsets(vector<int>& nums) {
    vector<vector<int>> ans;
    vector<int> path;
    backtrack(0, nums, path, ans);
    return ans;
}

