Time Complexity
O(n * 2^n): There are 2^n total subsets generated, and copying each subset of average length O(n) into the answer list takes O(n) time.

Space Complexity
O(n): The recursion call stack and the path buffer require up to O(n) auxiliary space (excluding the output storage of 2^n subsets).

Why the optimal solution is better than the brute-force approach
A naive generate-and-filter brute-force approach might generate duplicate permutations or non-unique subsets and require an expensive hash set lookup to filter out unique sets, costing extra time and memory. The backtracking approach generates each of the 2^n unique subsets exactly once without redundant branches, checking, or set-lookup overhead.
